.TH MEMORY 3
.SH NAME
memccpy, memchr, memcmp, memcpy, memmove, memset, bcopy, bcmp, bzero \- memory operations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <memory.h>

char *
memccpy(s1, s2, c, n)
char *s1, *s2;
int c;
unsigned n;

char *
memchr(s, c, n)
char *s;
int c;
unsigned n;

int
memcmp(s1, s2, n)
char *s1, *s2;
unsigned n;

char *
memcpy(s1, s2, n)
char *s1, *s2;
unsigned n;

char *
memmove(s1, s2, n)
char *s1, *s2;
unsigned n;

char *
memset(s, c, n)
char *s;
int c;
unsigned n;

bcopy(s2, s1, n)
char *s2, *s1;
unsigned n;

int
bcmp(s1, s2, n)
char *s1, *s2;
unsigned n;

bzero(s, n)
char *s;
unsigned n;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions work on areas of memory of
.I n
bytes, which may hold anything, NULs included. <string.h> declares
them as well.
.TP 10
.B memcpy
copies
.I n
bytes from the area
.I s2
to the area
.IR s1 ,
and returns
.IR s1 .
The areas must not overlap.
.TP
.B memmove
copies in the same way, and the areas may overlap: the copy is made as
if through a third area.
.TP
.B memccpy
copies bytes from
.I s2
to
.IR s1 ,
stopping after the first byte equal to
.I c
has been copied, or after
.I n
bytes. It returns a pointer to the byte after the copy of
.I c
in
.IR s1 ,
or NULL if
.I c
was not among the
.I n
bytes.
.TP
.B memchr
returns a pointer to the first byte equal to
.I c
in the
.I n
bytes at
.IR s ,
or NULL if there is none.
.TP
.B memcmp
compares the areas byte by byte, as unsigned numbers, and returns a
number less than, equal to, or greater than 0, as
.I s1
is less than, equal to, or greater than
.IR s2 .
.TP
.B memset
sets each of the
.I n
bytes at
.I s
to
.IR c ,
and returns
.IR s .
.TP
.B bcopy
copies
.I n
bytes from
.I s2
to
.IR s1 ;
note that the source comes first. The areas may overlap.
.TP
.B bcmp
returns 0 if the areas are the same, and 1 if not.
.TP
.B bzero
sets the
.I n
bytes at
.I s
to zero.
.SH SEE ALSO
string(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.BR memccpy ,
.BR memchr ,
.BR memcmp ,
.B memcpy
and
.B memset
functions first appeared in System V. The
.BR bcopy ,
.B bcmp
and
.B bzero
functions first appeared in 4.2BSD.
.PP
.B memmove
is not in the C libraries of Version 7 AT&T UNIX, System V or 4.3BSD.
